course: dictionary
lesson: 13235
title: Generalship: leading and planning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A general is the leader of an army. Generalship is the skill that makes a good general: planning the battles and leading the soldiers. The ending -ship often means a skill or a state, as in leadership and friendship.
explain: Look at the words around it to find the meaning. «Her generalship won the battle.» Won and battle are clues that this is about fighting, and planning, so generalship must mean clever leading and planning of the army.
explain: The word is not only for armies. «The captain's generalship kept the whole team working together.» A captain has no army, but planning and leading a big task well is still generalship. Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence: "The captain's clever planning and leading kept the team together" still makes sense.
fact: Generalship means the skill of a general in leading an army and planning battles.
fact: Generalship can also mean clever leadership and planning of any big task.
fact: The ending -ship often shows a skill or a state, as in leadership.
fact: Generalship is an uncountable noun, so you say "great generalship", not "a generalship".
fact: In The Wind in the Willows, Rat organises the attack to win back Toad Hall, so he shows generalship.
exercise: «Her generalship won the battle.» What does generalship mean here? | Singing loudly to the soldiers | Being very tall and strong | Carrying heavy things across the field | *Clever leading and planning of the army
exercise: Which word is the base of generalship? | Army | *General | Ship | Plan
exercise: «Rat showed great generalship when he planned the attack on Toad Hall.» Which word tells you the planning is for a battle? | Rat | Showed | *Attack | Toad
test: «The captain's generalship kept the whole team working together.» What does generalship mean? | Shouting at the referee | Owning a large ship | *Skilled planning and leading of a big task | Being bad at games
test: Which character in The Wind in the Willows shows generalship? | Mole, who is asleep | *Rat, who organises the attack on Toad Hall | Badger, who eats his dinner | Toad, who sings songs
exam: Which best explains generalship? | Being good at drawing pictures | Speed on the football pitch | *Clever leadership and planning of a big task | Learning to speak a new language
